  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Central American Educational Hub for Effective Policy-making</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">As a follow-up initiative to enhance public policy capacity and educational cooperation in Guatemala, this project leverages the collaborative framework between the KDI School of Public Policy and Management and the Regional School of Public Policy for Development (ERPPD). The project aims to enhance the existing Masters in Public Policy (MPP) program and establish a new Masters in Public Management (MPM) program by strengthening faculty capabilities, developing curricula, stabilizing institutional operations, and fostering an academic network.  

   In the short term, the project will focus on developing the MPM curriculum, operating the degree program, and strengthening cooperation among universities affiliated with the Central American University Superior Council (CSUCA). In the long term, it is expected to contribute to improving public service efficiency, strengthening governance capacity in Central America, and expanding the scope of Korea-Central America cooperation. To achieve these goals, continuous monitoring and strengthened collaboration with the local government will be essential.  

   The project is structured as a five-year initiative with a phased implementation plan. In the first year, efforts will be made to establish a foundation for collaboration through an invitational training program for policy decision-makers. The second year will focus on enhancing faculty capacity, developing curricula, and completing the necessary administrative procedures to launch the degree program. The third year will mark the official launch of the Masters in Public Management (MPM) program, followed by the fourth year, which will focus on refining the program and improving the curriculum based on evaluations and feedback. By the fifth year, the project aims to produce the first cohort of MPM graduates, ensuring the sustainability of the program and expanding its regional impact.  

   Through this structured approach, the project seeks to establish ERPPD as a hub for public policy education in Central America, strengthening the capacity of public sector officials and fostering a sustainable model for governance and policy implementation in the region.</narrative>
  </description>
